# frisian-mcp-demo — NetBox host: annotated reference.
#
# YOU DO NOT NEED TO COPY THIS FILE. `.env` is committed and holds working
# defaults; `docker compose up` works on a fresh clone with no setup step. This
# file exists to explain what each setting does, and to document the ones that
# are dangerous to change.
#
# Settings are grouped by how much damage a wrong value does.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CHANGE THESE ONLY DELIBERATELY — they are controls, not defaults to tidy up
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The host interface published ports bind to.
#
# THIS IMAGE SHIPS PUBLISHED CREDENTIALS. Every token, password and key it uses
# is printed in this repository. Setting this to 0.0.0.0 does not "expose the
# demo to your LAN" — it publishes an administrative account whose password is
# in a public git repository, to everything that can route to this machine.
#
# 0.0.0.0 is supported. It is not defaulted, and a README banner is not a
# control. Rotate every credential first if you intend to keep the instance.
DEMO_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Host port for the application. 8083 keeps it clear of the other demo hosts —
# 8080 is Nautobot, 8081 Paperless, 8082 Open edX — so all of them can run at
# once.
#
# Changing this ALSO changes the OAuth issuer, which docker-compose.yml derives
# from it. The issuer is echoed verbatim as `resource` in the RFC 9728 metadata
# and compared as a string, so it has to be the origin a client actually
# connects to. The redirect URIs the baked OAuth client accepts are in the
# database, not derived — so a browser OAuth connect on a non-default port is
# refused for an invalid redirect until that row is updated.
#
# Static Bearer tokens are unaffected. Only the browser flow cares.
DEMO_HTTP_PORT=8083

# Django's host header allowlist — a second gate behind the loopback bind.
# Reaching the demo under any other hostname fails at the Django layer even if
# DEMO_BIND_HOST is widened.
#
# SPACE-separated, not comma-separated. That is how the netbox-docker image
# parses it, and it differs from the Paperless host's comma-separated form.
#
# The image APPENDS "localhost" when neither "*" nor "localhost" is present, for
# its own healthcheck — so this list is a real restriction even though
# localhost is always reachable.
NETBOX_ALLOWED_HOSTS=127.0.0.1 localhost [::1]

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DO NOT CHANGE — changing these breaks the demo, silently
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The single tag both images resolve from.
#
# A database dump is welded to the migration state that produced it. An
# application image and a database image from different builds produce
# migration errors, or an estate the application cannot read.
#
# Unlike the Paperless host, this estate lives entirely in the dump — NetBox's
# demo objects are rows, not files on disk — so there is no media half to keep
# in step. The tags must still match.
#
# There is no supported combination in which the two tags differ.
DEMO_TAG=v0.1.0-pre

# The key the baked demo tokens are HMAC'd under.
#
# Tokens are stored as HMAC-SHA256(raw_token, key). Change this and every token
# in the baked database becomes unverifiable: the rows are still there, auth
# simply fails, and no log anywhere says why.
#
# It is published on purpose. The tokens it covers ship inside the image by
# design, so the key protects nothing that is not already public. Randomising
# it is not a hardening, it is a self-inflicted outage.
FRISIAN_MCP_HMAC_KEY=frisian-mcp-demo-public-hmac-key-do-not-reuse

# The database role the dump assigns ownership to.
#
# db/00-assert-role.sh refuses to restore under any other role, deliberately:
# the postgres entrypoint restores with ON_ERROR_STOP, so a mismatch is a
# FATAL, PARTIAL restore rather than a warning — and the reason ends up buried
# hundreds of lines into a container log.
POSTGRES_DB=netbox
POSTGRES_USER=netbox
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=netbox

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT — setting these breaks something
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# SECRET_KEY
#   netbox-docker REQUIRES one and does not generate it; config/frisian_mcp.py
#   generates it on first boot and persists it to the demo_state volume.
#
#   Baking a fixed key into a published image would publish a session-forgery
#   key for every copy of this demo running anywhere. Set it only if you have a
#   reason to pin it, and never to a published value.
#
#   Note the failure mode if it is missing entirely: the container reports
#   "Waiting on DB" forever rather than naming the missing key.
#
# SUPERUSER_NAME / SUPERUSER_PASSWORD / SUPERUSER_API_TOKEN
#   SKIP_SUPERUSER=true is set in docker-compose.yml. The demo's superuser is
#   `demo-admin`, and it arrives in the baked database with a documented
#   password. Setting these creates a SECOND superuser on every boot — one that
#   no README mentions and no assertion covers.
#
#   SUPERUSER_API_TOKEN is worse than redundant: it mints a NetBox REST API
#   token, which reaches the API directly and bypasses the MCP gateway that
#   this demo exists to show. db/assert-identities.sh asserts users_token is
#   empty for exactly that reason.
#
# FRISIAN_MCP_HEAVY_CACHE_URL
#   Opt-in, and off on purpose. Setting it silences frisian_mcp.W016 without
#   necessarily delivering the isolation W016 is about — two cache aliases on
#   different logical DBs of ONE Redis still share that instance's memory. The
#   full reasoning is in config/frisian_mcp.py.
#
#   The demo ships the honest warning instead. common/ci/acceptance-netbox.sh
#   allows W016 by ID and fails on every other frisian_mcp finding.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BUILD-ONLY — read by docker-compose.build.yml, ignored on the default path
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Exactly one of these two must be set when building the application image,
# and there is no default. A build with neither stops rather than resolving to
# a version nobody chose; a build with both stops rather than silently picking
# a lane.
#
# FRISIAN_MCP_SPEC=frisian-mcp[usage]==1.1.0
# FRISIAN_MCP_LOCAL_WHEEL=frisian_mcp-1.1.0-py3-none-any.whl
